=== task: multi hard
title: Choosing a storage
prompt: Choose **every** statement that is true.
* Checking whether u–v is an edge takes O(1) in an adjacency matrix :: Right. It's one look-up: `matrix[iu][iv]`.
* An adjacency list uses O(V + E) space :: Right. One list per vertex, plus one entry per edge end.
* Listing a vertex's neighbours from a matrix takes O(V) :: Right. You have to scan its whole row.
* An edge list needs a full scan to check for an edge :: Right. O(E): every pair might be the one.
- An adjacency matrix uses O(V + E) space :: It always has V × V boxes: O(V²), whatever the number of edges.
- An adjacency list checks for an edge in O(1) :: It scans one neighbour list: O(degree).
hint: How is each storage laid out in memory?
hint: For each question, how many items might have to be looked at?
right: Matrices answer "is u–v an edge?" in O(1) but cost O(V²) space; lists cost O(V + E) and take O(degree) to check; edge lists need a full scan.
wrong: A matrix has V² boxes but checks an edge with one look-up. Adjacency lists hold only real edges and scan one list to check. An edge list has to be scanned whole.

